{"uncertainty": 0.35, "outcome": "Triage report and three repro testbenches for the failing pwm_gen tests (pulse_width, period, duty_update). Reading the approved RTL suggests three separate causes, one per failing test, and each has its own repro. VeriTriage's own classification was low-confidence (unknown_failure, 30%), so the buckets come from reading the RTL source, not from the tool.", "artifacts": [{"kind": "triage_report", "title": "pwm_gen nightly regression triage: three buckets, three repros", "summary": "Serves [requirement:req-e946df186d]. VeriTriage session [run:run-0001] covered pulse_width_fail.log, period_fail.log and duty_update_fail.log in one call. It classified them as unknown_failure at 30% confidence, with 3 evidence nodes and a top hypothesis of 'Likely RTL bug'. That class is a lead, not a bucket. I compared that lead with the approved RTL [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1], whose review record is [evidence:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.e1], against the behaviour stated in its header comment. I found three separate departures, and each matches one failing test by name.\n\nBucket A, pulse_width: the line `pwm <= (cnt <= duty_now)` uses <= where < is intended. The output stays high for duty+1 cycles instead of duty. Signature: the high-run length is one more than duty while duty is constant. Repro: tb_pwm_pulse_width.v.\n\nBucket B, period: the line `if (cnt == PERIOD - 2) cnt <= 0` wraps the counter one count early. The period becomes PERIOD-1 = 7 cycles instead of 8. Signature: the gap between rising edges is 7 instead of 8, whatever the duty. Repro: tb_pwm_period.v.\n\nBucket C, duty_update: `duty_now` is wired to the live `duty` input, and the sampled `duty_q` is never used. A duty change in the middle of a period therefore takes effect at once instead of in the next period. Signature: after a mid-period duty change, the current period's high run differs from the earlier periods. Repro: tb_pwm_duty_update.v.